Why AGPL rather than MIT or GPL:

Nex's entire premise is that no server carries your messages and no third party
can read them. Under a permissive licence, someone could take this code, remove
those properties, and run it as a hosted service without ever showing anyone the
change. Section 13 — Remote Network Interaction — is what closes that: a
modified version offered to users over a network must offer its source too.

This does not forbid commercial use. It requires that changes stay visible.
